WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Good morning Hank, It's Tuesday!

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.720
So, you may have noticed that as years go 2016 has been, you know, something of a dumpster fire

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.180
and yet there is still a lot of good news around the world.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.060
We tend to hear about the bad news a lot because it happens suddenly and it is often dramatic,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.860
where as, good news usually happens slowly.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:24.020
like as Max Roser from "Our World In Data" put it you never see headlines reading: Global Child Mortality Fell By 0.00719% Since Yesterday.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.640
But, there is lots of encouraging news!

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:26.500
Like, let's start there actually,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:34.960
Child mortality continues to fall around the world and 2016 is on pace to have the fewest child deaths of any year in like thousands of years.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.760
Literally!

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.400
Then there is Ebola, remember the Ebola virus outbreak that killed almost 11 thousand people in Guinea, Sierra Leone, and Liberia?

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:47.940
It has ended, although flare ups remain possible, and strengthening community health centers in those countries is vital to prevent future outbreaks.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.840
Speaking of ending, we are very very close to ending the debilitating, and extremely painful Guinea worm disease.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:02.560
There were about 3.5 million cases as recently as 1986 and in 2015 there were 22 cases, so far in 2016 there have been 7.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:10.880
And then there is Malaria which continues to be a massive global health problem, but the attention and funding it's received in the past 15 years has made a huge difference.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.700
Around the world, Malaria deaths have fallen by almost half since 2000.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.240
We are also making progress when it comes to non-infectious diseases like, a large phase 3 study just found a better treatment for pancreatic cancer,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.840
and early stage pancreatic cancer survival rates are now approaching 30%, where as almost a decade ago they were under 10%.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:33.560
And overall cancer survival rates are also going up, like in the US in 1980 the overall cancer survival rate was just under 50%.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.200
Today it is very close to 70%. In fact,  it might even reach that milestone this year.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:45.440
Okay, let's move on to war. So worldwide conflict deaths were down in 2015 from 2014 and they are on pace to be down again this year.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.700
This is largely due to decreases in violence in Afghanistan and from  Boko Haram insurgency.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:56.000
I wanna be clear though that 2016 is still a definite dumpster fire when it comes to conflict, it's just that it was worse in 2015,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.920
and worse than that in 2014.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:05.600
but, it is worth noting that worldwide percentage of humans who die in war has gone way way way down since 1945.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:12.400
Also, ISIS is losing. They have lost the cities of Fallujah and Manbij, and while they definitely remain a threat especially in Syria, Iraq, and Libya

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.000
to quote The Economist,  "The self-proclaimed Islamic state is weakening fast."

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.980
Okay, let's move on to bankruptcy, there is less of it, at least in the US.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:27.020
In fact,  the rate of personal bankruptcies has fallen every year since 2010 and is down again in 2016.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:34.620
Meanwhile, the global economy is growing and will probably grow faster this year than it did last year, and worldwide absolute poverty is declining.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:42.280
In fact, in 2016 there are 200 million fewer people living in absolute poverty, then there were just 4 years ago in 2012.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.680
Literacy rates are going up around the world and the literary gender gap is shrinking.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.200
The percentage of literate humans will be higher than every this year, and no, people have not stopped reading books.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.760
In fact, sales in bookstores are up in 2016. It's the first time they've risen in many years and nobody knows quite why it's happening,

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.760
but it reminds me that I should really finish that book that I've been working on.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:06.980
We're gonna end with a few fun ones: Video games are getting better. Like, I know lots of people don't like it, but I am overwhelmed by the beauty and size of

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:13.780
the new video game: No Man's Sky. In fact, Hank, I've just began a new series on HankGames where I explore my own existential anxiety,

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.780
while walking around a largely abandoned planet. You can watch that by clicking here, or there is a link in the doobly-doo.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.240
but, in general video games have gotten awesome.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.360
What else? We got more Harry Potter, AFC Wimbledon is in league One and our awesome new advertising is up in their stadium.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.300
Pokemon Go has lead me to explore a fascinating and intricately detailed world called outside.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.220
And Beyonce released maybe the best non-mountain goats album of the decade.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:41.060
Hank, again I'm not trying to say that everything is going well here on Earth, there is much to lament and much that needs to change,

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:48.420
but I'm really encouraged that as a species we've shown the ability to make broad positive changes, and I really believe that in spite of it all,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.920
hope is not misguided. Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

